Overlay patch 0051: THE ALYX ITEM COMPASS (VR R6, Q-VR12).

Donor: ShipWright-VR `soh/soh/Enhancements/vr-combat/VrItemSelect.cpp`
(branch MotionControls-2), 573 lines; VR-DONOR-MAP section 8. R5's mining
sharpened the estimate that made this affordable: the compass draws through the
GAME's own display lists -- `gItemIcons`, `Matrix_Translate`, a POLY_XLU branch
at play-draw-end -- with no PCVR dependency anywhere in the drawing path. What
it needs from the platform is a hand pose and a button, and we have both.

HOW IT WORKS. Hold the selector input on one controller; a compass of the
currently equipped items appears anchored where your hand was. FLICK the hand
toward one of them; RELEASE to take it. Up is the sword, left/down/right are the
three C items, no movement at all is empty hands. That is Valve's Half-Life:
Alyx weapon menu almost verbatim, and the donor's header records why Valve chose
it over holsters: it never misses and it never drops anything.

WHAT WE PORT, AND WHAT WE DELIBERATELY DO NOT.

Ported, from the donor almost line for line:
  * the availability predicate (VR first person, not flat, save loaded, not in a
    blocking cutscene, no transition running);
  * the anchor that RIDES LINK'S BODY -- captured as an offset from the body at
    hold-start and re-derived from the body every tick, so walking (with the
    stick or physically) carries the compass along instead of leaving it behind,
    and locomotion can never read as a flick;
  * head-relative sectors: the camera's RIGHT is captured once at open and
    flattened to the horizon, so "left" is left as you see it and stays there
    even if you turn your head mid-selection;
  * hysteresis -- come back inside 65 % of the threshold before it reads as "no
    pick", and a 15 % lead is required to leave the current sector, so the
    boundary between two adjacent directions cannot flicker;
  * the confirmation haptic on every highlight change;
  * execution through the REAL input path (an emulated C press, and vanilla's
    own put-away for the centre), so items behave exactly as if their button had
    been pressed and nothing about ammo, magic, bottles or aim-and-throw is
    re-implemented. R21 takes the SWORD sector off the emulated B and onto
    vanilla's own item change (`heldItemButton = 0; Player_UseItem(buttonItems
    [0])`, which is Player_ProcessItemButtons's entire response to that press),
    because the donor's `Player_GetMeleeWeaponHeld == 0` gate reads 4 for a Deku
    stick and 5 for the Megaton hammer and so pressed NOTHING with either in
    hand;
  * the billboarded icon quads, the selected icon's 1.35x scale and full-bright
    prim colour, the dim placeholder diamond for an empty slot, and the centre
    ring marker -- all of it, including the donor's exact colours.

NOT ported, and each for a stated reason:
  * The SELECTOR MODE input rules -- both triggers reserved outright, C presses
    forbidden from changing the held item, the two-grip quick-swap chord, the
    padmgr binding profile that pays for those reservations. The brief for this
    round is explicit that R4's trigger-mirrors-the-item's-button setting STAYS
    as the fallback, and it does: `gSohVRItemTriggerMask` is untouched. Taking
    the donor's ownership rules as well would re-bind half the controller in a
    round where nobody has yet held one of these controllers.
  * The ocarina stand-down. It exists in the donor to keep the selector's
    RESERVATIONS from eating a note; we reserve nothing, so there is nothing to
    stand down. The compass still refuses to open during a cutscene or a
    transition, which is where it would actually be in the way.

THE DRAW SITE. The donor hooks `OnPlayDrawEnd`. We call the same function from
the same moment in `z_play.c`'s `Play_Draw`, immediately after the actor draw
pass and before the overlay is chained -- POLY_XLU is open, the world matrix
stack is where the donor's is, and (this is the part that matters for VR) it is
INSIDE the pass overlay 0031 runs per eye, so the compass gets real per-eye
disparity like everything else in the world rather than landing at identical
pixels the way the HUD does.

FRAME INTERPOLATION. The anchor rides in a RECORDED matrix, exactly as the donor
has it, so LUS glides the compass at render rate with the world instead of
stepping it at the 20 Hz tick. Without the record the compass is the one thing
on screen that visibly stutters, because it is the one thing anchored to a hand.

DESKTOP AND EVERY NON-VR BUILD. The whole file is `#ifdef __IOS__`, the call
sites are `#ifdef __IOS__`, and the gate is `gSohVRItemSel && gSohVRMode &&
gSohVRFpActive && !gSohVRFlatLatch` -- state a flat window, a 3D-panel session
and every iPhone build never set.

R18 PART C -- THE WHEEL DRAWS 3D MODELS (D-070). the user, on 1.0.1.22: "can our
weapon wheel be improved? They look like 2D models -- could we use 3D models
that look better?" It could, and this is that change: each occupied slot is now
the game's own spinning "get item" model (`GetItem_Draw`), the 2D `gItemIcons`
quad surviving as the fallback for a slot with no model, for an empty slot, and
for the whole wheel when `vr set wheel3d 0`. THIS IS AN IMPROVEMENT OVER THE
DONOR, NOT A PORT OF IT: ShipWright-VR's VrItemSelect.cpp draws the same flat
32x32 icons we did.

Why it is affordable at all: in SoH a get-item display list is an OTR PATH
STRING resolved through LUS's hash table at draw time (`gGiBowDL` is
`static const char[] "__OTR__objects/object_gi_bow/gGiBowDL"`, and the exported
resource carries CRC64 references, not segmented addresses), so drawing one
needs NO object load and NO segment 6 -- unlike the N64, where
`Player_DrawGetItemImpl` has to point segment 6 at `giObjectSegment` first. The
draw funcs set the segments they do need (8/9 tex scroll) themselves.

What the wheel has to supply, copied from `Actor_Draw` and `func_8002EBCC`,
because these models are LIT and Z-TESTED where the icon quads were neither:
scene lights bound at the wheel's anchor (`LightContext_NewLights`,
`Lights_BindAll`, `Lights_Draw`), and the hilite/lookat pair
(`func_8002EABC`/`func_8002EB44` with `envCtx.dirLight1`'s direction) that
En_GirlA and EnItem00's 3D drops set up the same way. Each slot then gets its
own `FrameInterpolation_RecordOpenChild(sSohSelDl, 1 + i)` so all four spin
smoothly instead of stepping at 20 Hz.

THE SIZE, MEASURED RATHER THAN GUESSED. There is no per-GID scale table in the
game, and the shop's `Actor_SetScale(0.25f)` is not transferable: a shop
pedestal has a room to itself, and our four slots sit on a ring of radius
`ringR` = 4.5 game units at the shipped 5 cm flick distance. So the vertex data
decided it. Reading every `objects/object_gi_*` Vtx resource out of the
extracted O2R, the bounding boxes cluster tightly: max extent 45-80 raw units,
typical 65 (bow 63, bottle 53, hammer 61, boomerang 60, hookshot 65, ocarina
56, kokiri sword 56, biggoron's 64). EnItem00's "NewDrops" enhancement confirms
the same normal from the other side -- its effective scales (actor scale x its
own `Matrix_Scale`) are 0.21 to 0.42 across a dozen GIDs. So one base scale
does work, and it is derived from the ring instead of copied from the shop:

    giScale = ringR * 0.0135 * gSohVRWheelScale

which puts a typical 65-unit model at about 0.62 of the 6.4-unit gap between
adjacent slots -- 0.061 at the shipped ring, a quarter of the shop's 0.25
because our ring is a quarter of a shop's spacing. `vr set wheelscale`
(default 1.0, clamped 0.02 .. 4.0) is the dial, because a number derived from
vertex bounds is still not a number anyone has WORN.

Two models depart from the 65-unit norm enough to need a factor, and both
factors are ratios of measured extents: the gold skull token (object_gi_sutaru,
144) and the Zora mask (object_gi_zoramask, 106). The Deku egg (80) gets one
too. Everything else rides the base.

THE RING AND THE FLICK DISTANCE DO NOT MOVE. The brief for this round suggested
shrinking them; measurement says otherwise, and the reason is worth recording.
Pulling the ring in cannot help occlusion -- the anchor's depth in the world is
unchanged by its radius -- and it would both crowd four models that are far
bigger than four 3.2-unit quads AND move where the wearer's hand has to flick,
which is the one thing in this feature that the user's hands have already learned.
The size problem is solved by the scale term instead.

ITEM_SWORD_MASTER IS THE ONE HOLE, AND SoH ITSELF FILLS IT. The GID enum has no
master sword: vanilla never hands you one as a get-item. SoH's randomizer wants
one anyway and draws `object_toki_objects_DL_001BD0` -- the pedestal sword --
in `Randomizer_DrawMasterSword` (Enhancements/randomizer/draw.cpp). The wheel
uses that DL through five lines of the same code in C (rather than calling into
the randomizer's C++ TU with a NULL GetItemEntry), and its extra factor is a
measurement, not a copy: that DL's vertices span 1670 units, so 65/1670 = 0.039
puts it on screen at the same size as its neighbours, where the randomizer's own
0.05 would draw it 28 % large.

THE SELECTED SLOT. `SETUPDL_25` uses the prim colour as the fog term, so the
donor's full-bright-prim trick cannot brighten a lit model. The selected slot is
therefore a scale change plus a billboard marker behind it, drawn through the
existing quad path. The marker is the one quad in this file that IS depth tested
(`G_RM_ZB_XLU_SURF`) and is pushed back along the camera's forward by the model's
own half-extent, so the model occludes its middle and what shows is a rim of
light around it.

R19 PART B -- THE MARKER IS A RING, NOT A SQUARE (D-073). the user, on 1.0.1.23:
"the highlight is a big yellow square -- not pretty. A subtle border around the
object, or a subtle highlight of the object itself." Both halves of that are
taken:

  * THE SQUARE WAS LITERALLY A SQUARE. R18 part C's marker was an UNTEXTURED
    `G_CC_PRIMITIVE` quad -- so every one of its pixels was prim colour, corners
    included -- at prim (255, 214, 130, 150) and half-size `half * 1.6`, which at
    the shipped scale is about 8.6 units across against a 6.4-unit gap between
    adjacent slots. A softly glowing rim was the intent; a solid yellow tile the
    size of the slot spacing was the result.
  * THE RING. `gLensFlareRingTex` (gameplay_keep, i4 64x64) IS an annulus -- read
    out of the extracted archive, it is a thin bright band at radius ~30 texels
    with a hollow centre and nothing in the corners -- and vanilla's own lens
    flare draws it with exactly the combiner this wants (`z_kankyo.c` ~1565:
    colour = PRIMITIVE, alpha = TEXEL0 * PRIMITIVE). It is an OTR path string,
    so it is drawable anywhere without an object load, the same property the
    get-item models rely on. `SohVrSel_PushQuad` therefore takes a texcoord
    extent (31 for the 32x32 icons and the centre marker, 63 for the ring), the
    marker shrinks to `half * 1.25`, and the prim becomes a dimmer, warmer
    (255, 236, 186, 105).
  * THE BREATH. The flat 1.35x jump is replaced by `1.12 + 0.05 * sin(frames *
    0.35)` -- a gentle 1.07 .. 1.17 pulse at about 0.7 Hz on the tick clock -- so
    the selected slot reads as alive rather than merely bigger, and it no longer
    crowds its neighbours. The 2D icon fallback takes the same 1.12 without the
    pulse.
  * THE DIAL. `vr set wheelhalo`: 0 none (the breath alone), 1 ring (default),
    2 a soft round glow (`gUnknownCircle6Tex`, i8 32x32, the same combiner).
    Reported by `vr item`.

R20 -- NO MARKER AT ALL, AND A BIGGER MODEL (D-075). the user, on 1.0.1.24: "in
the right grip item wheel, let's remove the orange circle that surrounds your
selection. Let's just make the size or zoom of the object you're selecting a
little bigger. That's enough UI feedback for the user along with the haptics
that are already there." Both halves are taken literally and nothing else moves:

  * `gSohVRWheelHalo` SHIPS 0. The ring, the glow, the texcoord extent and every
    line of the marker path survive untouched behind the dial, so `vr set
    wheelhalo 1` is still a one-command A/B back to R19's ring. The default is
    the only thing that changed, and it changed in the one place the shell
    defines it (SohIosShell.m).
  * THE BREATH GROWS TO 1.35 +/- 0.05, bounds 1.30 .. 1.40, and the 2D icon
    fallback takes the 1.35 midpoint. That is R18's original jump restored as
    the CENTRE of the pulse rather than as a step: the reason R19 shrank it to
    1.12 was that the ring was sized off it and a ring at 1.35 crowded the
    neighbouring slots -- with no ring there is nothing to crowd. The clearance
    arithmetic is beside SohVrSel_SelScale: 1.7 units of gap at 1.35, 1.6 at the
    top of the breath.

WHAT WAS RULED OUT, AND IT IS NOT SPECULATION. The prettiest option would be a
SECOND pass of the model itself -- an outline hull, or the same model drawn
brighter. It is infeasible here for the reason recorded above and re-checked this
round: `GetItem_Draw` runs `Gfx_SetupDL_25*`, whose prim colour IS the fog term,
so a second pass cannot be tinted through prim without fogging the model, and
the get-item display lists set their own combiners internally. That is why the
marker is a separate textured quad and not a treatment of the model.

